#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293 :topic/math-academy-id 803 :topic/title "Graphing Curves Defined Parametrically"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362 :topic/math-academy-id 271 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Sine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703 :topic/math-academy-id 831 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784 :topic/math-academy-id 116 :topic/title "Solving Radical Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/math-academy-id 487 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048836 :topic/math-academy-id 3857 :topic/title "Alternate Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050107 :topic/math-academy-id 1455 :topic/title "Simplifying Trigonometric Expressions Using the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :topic/id #uuid "de5636f1-a352-5442-9fc2-8f1ec6ed0666" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100713 :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1255 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048858 :topic/math-academy-id 873 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Horizontal Hyperbolas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048918 :topic/math-academy-id 2981 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Vertical Hyperbolas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212 :topic/math-academy-id 3145 :topic/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722 :topic/math-academy-id 806 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Circles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050745 :topic/math-academy-id 6355 :topic/title "Clairaut Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050769 :topic/math-academy-id 875 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Parabolas"}] :topic/title "Cartesian Equations of Parametric Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370 :topic/math-academy-id 6678 :topic/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :topic/id #uuid "de6ce0f8-a513-5c11-b2ac-9d3484be570b" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :topic/math-academy-id 6679 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049028 :topic/math-academy-id 2973 :topic/title "Variation of Parameters for First-Order Linear ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050801 :topic/math-academy-id 3189 :topic/title "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050805 :topic/math-academy-id 882 :topic/title "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Exponential Forcing"}] :topic/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051 :topic/math-academy-id 2310 :topic/title "Compound Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050015 :topic/math-academy-id 3709 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions With Surds"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404 :topic/math-academy-id 2204 :topic/title "Surds"}] :topic/id #uuid "df4f6208-5d6c-5dc9-bd38-c7d85e32fb73" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/id #uuid "9096883e-6dd0-5dd1-8e9c-176775edadba" :question/math-academy-id "e-10785" :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, 5\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Open Intervals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/id #uuid "b828f144-b8b8-5eda-94f1-43cccdcb1aaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10786" :question/problem "Write the interval $y\\in\\big(-4, \\sqrt{2}\\big]$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,$.\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/id #uuid "45d05c7d-e278-552f-a524-926d4a57d870" :question/math-academy-id "e-10787" :question/problem "What interval corresponds to the segment shown below?\n\n![](images/43/430481f9d0863b0bb5602f949e6f994d8007ea29fdd741a485d880df8d5d2dec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816" :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Closed Intervals"}] :topic/math-academy-id 239 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048793 :topic/math-academy-id 6287 :topic/title "Optimizing Sums, Differences, Products, and Quotients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050301 :topic/math-academy-id 4028 :topic/title "Unbounded Intervals"}] :topic/title "Interval Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049228 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048209 :topic/math-academy-id 2625 :topic/title "The Least Common Multiple of Two Monomials"}] :topic/id #uuid "e51810dc-b223-5965-a75f-46a4335d3da1" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152116 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151877 :question/id #uuid "78763507-6493-5a57-91a1-1c0c92324473" :question/math-academy-id "e-14805" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $4(x+1)$ and $6(x+1).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $4\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 4, \\, 8, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $6\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\, 18, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6) =  {\\color{blue}{12}}.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+1), \\, (x+1)\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+1)}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(4(x+1), \\, 6(x+1)\\right) = {\\color{blue}{12}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+1)}} = 12(x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Polynomials that Differ by a Constant Factor"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152117 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151879 :question/id #uuid "588a1460-6cd3-519a-ae98-7bc80c7a946c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14809" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $9(x-1)^4$ and $12(x-1)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $9 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 9, \\, 18, \\, 27, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 45, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $12 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 12, \\, 24, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 48, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12) =  {\\color{blue}{36}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x-1)^4$ and $(x-1)^2,$ we raise $(x-1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x-1)^4, \\, (x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(9(x-1)^4, \\, 12(x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{blue}{36}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}} = 36(x-1)^4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152118 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151880 :question/id #uuid "9a1f928c-ab43-5d93-b187-13889113c46f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14813" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $6(x+3)^2(y+1)^2$ and $9(x+3)(y+1)^4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(6,9)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $6\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, {\\color{blue}18}, \\, 24, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $9\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 9, \\, {\\color{blue}18}, \\, 27, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(6,9) =  {\\color{blue}{18}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+3)^2(y+1)^2$ and $(x+3)(y+1)^4,$ we find the largest powers for $(x+3)$ and $(y+1)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(x+3)^2$ and $(x+3),$ the largest power is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- For $(y+1)^2$ and $(y+1)^4,$ the largest power is ${\\color{red}{4}}.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+3)$ and $(y+1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+3)^2(y+1)^2, \\,(x+3)(y+1)^4\\right) =(x+3)^{\\color{blue}{2}}\\cdot (y+1)^{\\color{red}{4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(6(x+3)^2(y+1)^2, \\, 9(x+3)(y+1)^4\\right) = 18(x+3)^2 (y+1)^4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Multivariable Polynomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152119 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151881 :question/id #uuid "3b462bac-92e9-5328-812c-da59d33b07f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14815" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $12(x+2)^4 (y+1),$ $\\,9(x+2) (y+1)^2,$ and $6(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(12, 9, 6)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $12\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 12, \\, 24, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 48, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $9\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 9, \\, 18, \\, 27, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 45, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $6 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, 18, \\, 24, \\, 30, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(12, 9, 6) =  {\\color{blue}{36}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+2)^4 (y+1), (x+2) (y+1)^2,$ and $(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3,$ we find the largest powers for $(x+2)$ and $(y+1)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x+2)^4,(x+2),$ and $(x+2)^2,$ the largest power is ${\\color{blue}{4}}.$\n\n- For $(y+1),(y+1)^2,$ and $(y+1)^3,$ the largest power is ${\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+2)$ and $(y+1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+2)^4 (y+1), \\,(x+2) (y+1)^2,\\,(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3\\right) =(x+2)^{\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot (y+1)^{\\color{red}{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\text{LCM}(12(x+2)^{4}(y+1),\\,9(x+2)(y+1)^{2},\\,6(x+2)^{2}(y+1)^{3}) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (x+2)^{4}\\cdot (y+1)^{3} \\\\  & =36(x+2)^{4}(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Polynomials"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2626 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050211 :topic/math-academy-id 708 :topic/title "Advanced Rational Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050353 :topic/math-academy-id 6150 :topic/title "Adding Rational Expressions Using Polynomial Factorization"}] :topic/title "The Least Common Multiple of Two Polynomials"}]}
